<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Perth Amboy</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> Amboy is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a> at the mouth of the Raritan River on Raritan Bay. Scottish proprietors founded it in the 1680s as the capital of East Jersey, naming it after the Earl of Perth. After New Jersey was reunited as a royal colony in 1702, it shared the role of capital with Burlington until the American Revolution.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/perth-amboy.jpg" alt="Perth Amboy" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rafaelgarcia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:CourtHousePoliceHQPerthAmboy.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Perth Amboy lies in northeastern Middlesex County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> metropolitan area, and borders the Arthur Kill. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lenape">Lenape</a> called the point on which it stands Ompoge, meaning level ground or standing upright. The name Amboy descends from that word, and the colonists later joined it to the name of their settlement. Perth Amboy and South Amboy, across the Raritan River, are together called the Amboys, and the city is linked to Staten Island by the Outerbridge Crossing.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Scottish colonists settled the place around 1683, recruited to occupy the share of the East Jersey colony owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-barclay">Robert Barclay</a>, a Quaker who later became the province's absentee governor. They called it New Perth in honour of James Drummond, 4th Earl of Perth, one of the twelve associates of the company of Scottish proprietors. In 1686 it was designated capital of East Jersey, while Burlington was capital of West Jersey; after the two were united as a royal colony in 1702, the two cities alternated as capital of the province. A royal charter followed on 4 August 1718, and the New Jersey Legislature confirmed the city's status on 21 December 1784.</p><p>By the middle of the 19th century immigration and industry had transformed the city. Factories such as A. Hall and Sons Terra Cotta, Guggenheim and Sons and the Copper Works Smelting Company employed many residents, and the city became the tidewater terminal of the Lehigh Valley Railroad and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> shipping point. Immigrants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> dominated the factory jobs and formed close ethnic neighbourhoods such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> and Chickentown. The Perth Amboy Public Library, one of the first Carnegie libraries in the state, opened in 1903.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The waterfront keeps the Perth Amboy Ferry Slip, once an important crossing on the route south from New York City and listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1978, as well as the Proprietary House, the former governor's mansion, which now holds a museum. St. Peter's Episcopal Church and the Raritan Yacht Club, one of the oldest yacht clubs in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, also stand there. The 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 55,436 people, and most residents are Hispanic, with Puerto Rican and Dominican communities among them.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Perth Amboy, New Jersey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Scottish proprietors founded <b>Perth Amboy</b> as their capital, and Barclay served as absentee governor from 1682 to 1688.</blockquote><a href="/state/east-jersey">East Jersey</a></li><li><blockquote>The reunited royal colony shared its governor with the Province of New York until 1738, and its capital alternated between <b>Perth Amboy</b> and Burlington.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-new-jersey">Province of New Jersey</a></li></ul></section>
